# Runtime dependencies — what production needs to serve the site.
#
# This file is hand-written and holds intent. `requirements.txt` is generated
# from it and is the lockfile production installs; never edit that by hand.
#
#     pip-compile --no-emit-index-url --strip-extras requirements.in  # regenerate
#     pip-compile --upgrade-package django                            # move one deliberately
#     pip-sync requirements-dev.txt                                   # make this venv match
#
# Python 3.12, the version the PythonAnywhere web app runs -- not whatever is
# newest locally. The lockfile must be resolved with this interpreter too;
# resolving on 3.14 previously admitted a package set production could not
# install, while the server's user-level pip-sync runs under 3.13 and is not the
# web runtime either. See ../freetheplatform/_docs/strategy.md.
#
# Pins are the versions that are installed and passing tests, not the newest
# available. Every upgrade after that is a `--upgrade-package` with a diff
# someone can read.

# Capped below 6.1, which raised Django's minimum MySQL to 8.4. PythonAnywhere
# serves 8.0.42, so 6.1 refuses to open a connection at all:
# "MySQL 8.4 or later is required (found 8.0.42)". 6.0 still accepts 8.0.11+.
#
# This line was bare `Django`, and the lock -- resolved on a machine that never
# talks to production -- picked exactly 6.1. The cap is the production
# database's constraint written down, so a later recompile cannot quietly step
# over it. Lifting it means moving MySQL first, not the other way round.
Django<6.1
djangorestframework
djangorestframework-simplejwt
mysqlclient
python-dotenv

# Shared Django capabilities: agreements and messaging. The `twilio` extra is
# what the messaging app's SMS backend needs — nothing in this repository
# imports twilio or requests directly, so they arrive through here rather than
# being declared twice.
#
# Pinned to a tag, and a security fix in the package means a new tag and a bump
# here the same day. See ../freetheplatform/_docs/strategy.md.
freetheplatform[twilio,stripe,documents,searchconsole] @ git+https://github.com/ethanbetts63/freetheplatform@v0.32.0

# Dealer document uploads are sniffed by bytes and fully parsed before they are
# stored; see core/utils/uploads.py. `pypdf` also fills the prescribed AcroForms
# — VL17 and MR9B — and merges the signature overlay onto them.
Pillow
pypdf

# Typesetting the documents that have no published form to fill: the Schedule 5
# Vehicle Sale Contract, the Authority to Lodge, and the "Electronically signed
# by" overlay stamped onto a prescribed form. See
# _docs/licensing/plan/06-documents.md.
reportlab

stripe
